For a dagger to stack with other daggers it needs to have an
exact match with the other stack's:

enchantment number, whether the enchantment number is
know, b/u/c status, whether the b/u/c status is known,
type of dagger, level of damage.

So say you have two regular daggers where you don't know
what enchantment they have or if they are
blessed/uncursed/cursed.  It's how they look when you pick
them up.  It they stack you know they are the same buc
and the same +/- number plus unknown all the same.  If
they don't stack you know one or more is different.

Basically uncursed +0 is the most common so when you
have over a dozen the biggest stack will be that.  Drop
on an altar and more of them will stack because the
only unknown will be the +/- number.

This deal of only stacking if they have identical status
is deliberate.  The ones that don't stack aren't uncursed +0
so they are the interesting ones.  Once you know one that
does not stack isn't cursed, that's the one to wield because
it's either blessed and/or has a + enchantment.

Making an educated guess at the quality of a dagger
based only on if it stacks or not, if your pet steps on it
or not, that's a skill to be developed for the early game.

As to naming to keep them from stacking, it only sorta
works.  If two daggers with identical stats and identical
known-ness already have different names when you pick
them up they won't stack.  If they already have the same
names when you pick them up they will stack.  But if
you have a named one in your inventory and you pick up
an unamed one it will join a stack and take on the name
of the stack.  I like to name one "wield", another "engrave"
and the rest "quiver" - When I pick up one that would
stack it doesn't always the group you like.


